      "4. Wear and tear and the cost of chronic stress",
      "Sapolsky mentions Max Rubner’s early twentieth-century theory that every organism has a fixed metabolic energy allowance, or a lifetime quota of heartbeats. Although simplified, the wear-and-tear concept describes stress well. Chronic stress and persistent allostatic load accelerate bodily wear, raising the risk of diseases associated with ageing: type 2 diabetes, hypertension, osteoporosis and reduced immunity.",
      "5. Extreme ageing in the wild",
      "To illustrate how glucocorticoids can cause rapid ageing and death, Sapolsky discusses Pacific salmon and Australian marsupial mice. After spawning or mating, hormonal regulation fails and enormous amounts of glucocorticoids are released. Within days, immunity collapses, ulcers develop and the animals deteriorate dramatically. Removing the salmon’s adrenal source of glucocorticoids is described as extending life by a year. This illustrates how extreme stress-hormone exposure can implement programmed death.",
      "Chapter conclusion",
