      "3. Type A personality and worn-out chairs",
      "Type A individuals are competitive, impatient, hurried and hostile, with high cardiovascular risk. Cardiologists Friedman and Rosenman reportedly recognised this pattern through an upholsterer: their waiting-room chairs wore differently because patients perched on the edge and impatiently clawed the armrests. Researchers later found that hostility and cynicism, rather than haste alone, were central to the risk, especially when anger was outwardly suppressed, producing sharp blood-pressure rises.",
      "4. Repressive personality: the cost of a perfect facade",
      "About 5% of people appear entirely happy, successful and calm, without depression or anxiety, yet show chronically high glucocorticoids and weaker immunity. These are repressive personalities. Their calm reflects intense internal effort rather than relaxation. The frontal cortex works hard to suppress impulsive emotions and maintain a rigid, surprise-free world. Health pays the price for the composed facade.",
      "Chapter conclusion: How we perceive the world shapes physiology. Hostility, constant threat anticipation or strenuous emotional suppression can activate stress without an external danger. Distinguishing genuine from imagined threats and maintaining social ties, as Kenneth does, offer protection."
